#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180781 :tutorial/content "When solving a multivariable equation for a particular variable, we sometimes have to factor some terms in the equation to reach the solution.\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation for the variable $d$:\n\n$$\n2cd - 3d = 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor out $d$ in the expression on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nd(2c-3) = 5\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $(2c-3)$ to isolate $d$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(2c - 3) &= 5 \\\\\n(d(2c - 3))/(2c - 3) &= \\frac{5}{2c - 3} \\\\\n(d(2c - 3))/(2c - 3) &= \\frac{5}{2c - 3} \\\\\nd &= \\frac{5}{2c - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that\n\n$$\n2c-3\\neq 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46409ede-9b62-5196-b665-c5ba29062b0d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8665 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
